𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟴/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗛𝗶𝗴𝗵 𝗟𝗼𝗮𝗱 𝗔𝘃𝗲𝗿𝗮𝗴𝗲 𝗼𝗻 𝗟𝗶𝗻𝘂𝘅

🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“Your Linux server has a very high load average. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I would not immediately assume that high load means high CPU.

First, I would check:

uptime

or:

top

I would look at the 1, 5, and 15-minute load averages and compare them with the number of CPU cores.

Then I would check CPU usage:

top

and:

mpstat -P ALL 1

I would also check processes consuming CPU:

ps -eo pid,cmd,%cpu,%mem --sort=-%cpu | head

But high load can also happen because processes are waiting for disk I/O.

So I would check:

iostat -xz 1

and:

vmstat 1

I would look for high I/O wait, blocked processes, or unusually high disk activity.

Then I would investigate:

• Is CPU overloaded?
• Are processes waiting for disk I/O?
• Is there heavy disk activity?
• Is memory pressure causing swapping?
• Did traffic suddenly increase?
• Was there a recent deployment or batch job?
• Is a particular process consuming resources?

I would then identify the root cause before taking corrective action.

If a specific application or batch process is responsible, I would follow the approved production procedure rather than randomly killing processes.

After recovery, I would monitor the system and verify that the application is healthy.

Finally, I would document:

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

High load average does not automatically mean high CPU usage.

A good DevOps Engineer checks whether the system is limited by:

𝗖𝗣𝗨 → 𝗗𝗶𝘀𝗸 𝗜/𝗢 → 𝗠𝗲𝗺𝗼𝗿𝘆 → 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻
